SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 9/29/2026: Supreme Judicial Court to Hold Ora
 l Arguments at Three Maine High Schools
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nSupreme Judicial Court to Hold Oral Arguments a
 t Three Maine High Schools\nDate:\n9/29/2026\nThe Supreme Judicial Court w
 ill hold court and hear a total of nine cases at three Maine high schools 
 next week.\nOn Tuesday\, October 6\, beginning at 9:00 a.m.\, the Court wi
 ll visit Katahdin Middle/High School in Stacyville to hear arguments in th
 ree appellate cases. Senator Trey Stewart extended the invitation to the L
 aw Court. The three cases are scheduled to begin at 9:00 a.m.\, 10:00 a.m.
 \, and 11:00 a.m.\nThe Court will hear three appeals at Morse High School 
 in Bath on Wednesday\, October 7 at the invitation of Senator Denise Teple
 r. The Court will follow the same schedule with the first oral argument st
 arting at 9:00 a.m.\, the second at 10:00 a.m.\, and the third at 11:00 a.
 m.\nAnd on Thursday\, October 8\, the Court will hear three cases at Kenne
 bunk High School at the invitation of Representative Daniel Sayre. Again\,
  the schedule indicates that the first case starts at 9:00 a.m.\nStudents 
 receive copies of the briefs in advance and\, after each argument\, the la
 wyers stay to answer questions from interested students. When the cases ar
 e decided\, the schools receive a link to the decisions to share with the 
 students.\nBeginning in 2005\, the Court has held oral arguments in actual
  appellate cases at over 40 Maine high schools around the state. In each s
 chool\, the gymnasium\, cafeteria\, or auditorium is turned into a courtro
 om for the morning so that the students can observe the appellate process.
  The Court has visited schools from York County to Aroostook County\; from
  Oxford County to Washington County\; and many places in between.\nChief J
 ustice Valerie Stanfill said\, "We hope that providing real-time exposure 
 to the third branch of government supplements the students' civic\nhttps:/
 /www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13363659
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13363659
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
