BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:50f207a7851a5c29@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260926T045444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] United States Seeks Mexico’s Review of Alle
 ged Denial of Workers’ Rights at Yokohama Tire Manufacturing Mexico\, S.
 A. de C.V.
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The Office of the United States Trade Represent
 ative has invoked the Rapid Response Labor Mechanism (RRM) in the United 
 States-Mexico-Canada Agreement (USMCA) to review whether workers at Yokoh
 ama Tire Manufacturing Mexico\, S.A. de C.V. (Yokohama)\, located in the 
 state of Coahuila\, Mexico\, are being denied the right to freedom of asso
 ciation and collective bargaining. This follows the recent closure of the 
 Yokohama tire manufacturing plant in Salem\, Virginia and the layoff of ne
 arly 600 American workers. The United States will suspend the liquidation
  of all entries of goods into this country from the Yokohama facility\, 
 which produces tires\, until further notice.Today’s action demonstrates 
 the Trump administration’s America First approach\, which ensures our tr
 ade partners do not undermine worker protections to gain an unfair trade a
 dvantage or attract investment. The Secretary of Labor and United States 
 Trade Representative co-chair the Interagency Labor Committee for Monitori
 ng and Enforcement (ILC). On August 26\, 2026\, the ILC received an RRM p
 etition from the Liga Sindical Obrera Mexicana (LSOM)\, a Mexican labor un
 ion\, and the International Lawyers Assisting Workers Network (ILAW). The 
 petition alleges that Yokohama is violating workers’ rights to freedom o
 f association and collective bargaining by retaliating against workers for
  engaging in union activity. These actions include engaging in unequal tre
 atment against LSOM supporters\, the denial of access to the plant for LSO
 M special delegates while granting unrestricted access to the representati
 ves of a competing union\, irregularities leading up to a Certificate of R
 epresentation vote\, unlawful dismissals\, and non-compliance with the sec
 toral collective bargaining agreement for the rubber manufacturing industr
 y (known as the “contrato ley”). The ILC reviews RRM petitions that it
  receives\, and the accompanying information\, within 30 days.  After con
 ducting this review\, the ILC determined that there is\nhttp://www.dol.gov
 /newsroom/releases/ilab/ilab20260925
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ilab/ilab20260925
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3b6230cb85b912af@role-estate-probate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260926T045429Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] People Not Politicians v. Onder
DESCRIPTION:_________________ \n_________________ \n1  Cite as: 609 U. S. 
 ____ (2026) \nPer Curiam \nSUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES \nNo. 26A388
  \nPEOPLE NOT POLITICIANS\, ET AL. v. ROBERT \nONDER\, ET AL. \nON APPLICA
 TION FOR STAY \n[September 25\, 2026]\n PER CURIAM. \nIn 2022\, the Missou
 ri Legislature enacted a redistricting \nmap for elections to the U. S. Ho
 use of Representatives.  In \n2025\, the Missouri Legislature enacted a ne
 w map.  But \nunder the Missouri Constitution as interpreted by the \nSupr
 eme Court of Missouri\, a new law such as the 2025\nmap does not take effe
 ct unless and until approved by\nvoters in a referendum in the next electi
 on—at least where\, \nas here\, the number of voter signatures on the pe
 tition\nsuffices to trigger a referendum.  Therefore\, on September \n3\, 
 2026\, the Missouri Supreme Court ruled that the 2025 \nredistricting map 
 “is not the law and has never been the \nlaw” in Missouri—and will n
 ot take effect unless and until \napproved by Missouri voters by referendu
 m in the 2026 \nelection. von Glahn v. Hoskins\, ___ S. W. 3d ___\, ___–
 ___\, \nn. 8 (Mo. 2026)\, App. to Emergency Motion 53–54a\, n. 8.\nIn sh
 ort\, as a matter of state law\, the 2022 map—not the\n2025 map—must b
 e used in the 2026 congressional\nelection. \nIn the wake of that recent M
 issouri Supreme Court\ndecision\, a group of voters and candidates (collec
 tively\,\nplaintiffs) sued in federal cou rt.  They contended that the\nU.
  S. Constitution requires Missouri to use the 2025 map in \nthe 2026 elect
 ion—even though the 2025 map cannot take\neffect under Missouri law unle
 ss and until it is approved by\nMissouri voters in the 2026 election. \n  
 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n   \n   \n \
 n \n  \n \n2 PEOPLE NOT POLITICIANS v. ONDER \nPer Curiam \nIn two orders 
 in the last few weeks\, this Court has denied \nsuch relief. In the first\
 , the Missouri Secretary of State\nasked this Court to stay the September 
 3 decision of the\nMissouri Supreme Court that had prohibited use of the\n
 2025 map in the general election. On September 8\nhttps://www.supremecourt
 .gov/opinions/25pdf/26a388_q86b.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/26a388_q86b.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
